<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emily Dickinson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Emily Elizabeth Dickinson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emily Dickinson was an American poet, born in 1830 in Amherst, Massachusetts, to a prominent family, who was largely unpublished and unknown during her lifetime. She studied at the Amherst Academy for seven years and briefly at the Mount Holyoke Female Seminary, and from about 1867 rarely left the family Homestead. Of her nearly 1,800 poems, written in short lines with lean descriptions and slant rhyme and dealing mainly with nature and mortality, only 10 were published while she lived. Her sister Lavinia found the poems after her death; a selection of 1890 altered them heavily, and Thomas H. Johnson's complete edition of 1955 restored them closer to her intention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emily-dickinson.jpg" alt="Emily Dickinson" width="330" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-white_photograph_of_Emily_Dickinson2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dickinson was born on 10 December 1830 at the family homestead in Amherst, into a prominent but not wealthy family. Her father, Edward Dickinson, was a lawyer and treasurer of Amherst College from 1835 to 1873, and her grandfather Samuel Dickinson was one of the college's founders and built the Homestead in 1813. She had an older brother, Austin, and a younger sister, Lavinia. At the Amherst Academy she took classes in English and classical literature, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, botany, geology, history and arithmetic, and her principal remembered her as &quot;very bright&quot;. Benjamin Franklin Newton sent her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a>'s poems in 1850.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her strongest bond in the 1850s was with Susan Gilbert, who married Austin in 1856. In April 1862 Dickinson wrote to the critic Thomas Wentworth Higginson, asking: &quot;Are you too deeply occupied to say if my Verse is alive?&quot; In 1858 she began to gather her poems in small hand-sewn books she called fascicles. Her most productive period ran from 1861 to 1865, and Thomas H. Johnson estimated that she composed 366 poems in 1862 alone. She avoided pentameter and favoured the ballad stanza, with frequent slant rhyme, and her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> use dashes and unconventional capitalisation.</p><p>She was known in her lifetime more widely as a gardener than as a poet, and assembled a leather-bound herbarium of 424 pressed specimens. Only ten poems and a letter were published while she lived, anonymously and heavily edited, among them &quot;Nobody knows this little rose&quot; in Samuel Bowles's Springfield Republican; the last, &quot;Success is counted sweetest&quot;, appeared in A Masque of Poets in the 1870s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her father died on 16 June 1874 and her mother on 14 November 1882, and the next year Austin and Susan's youngest child, Gilbert, died of typhoid fever. In her last years she stopped editing and organising her poems and made Lavinia promise to burn her papers. She died on 15 May 1886. Lavinia burned most of the correspondence but found about 1,800 poems in a locked chest and, with Mabel Loomis Todd, who was Austin's lover, arranged their publication. A feud between the Todd and Dickinson houses divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and prevented complete publication for more than half a century.</p><p>The first volume, edited by Todd and Higginson in November 1890, regularised punctuation and capitalisation and occasionally reworded the poems; its 115 poems went through eleven printings in two years. Johnson's 1955 complete edition restored her work closer to her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and she has remained in print since 1890.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emily Dickinson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
